{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was the last Count of Tripoli from the House of Toulouse he ruled from 1192 until his death in 1202. His reign was marked by his involvement in conflicts with the Ayyubid dynasty and crusader states in the Levanter region.\n\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Eschiva of Bures. His early life remains largely undocumented but he would have been raised in the context of the Crusader States which were under constant threat from Muslim powers. His father Raymond II had been a key figure in the Crusades and had been a regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nUpon inheriting the countship Raymond III found himself in a precarious position. His predecessor his father had died without a male heir other than Raymond III himself. The Crusader States were in a state of decline and the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin had made significant gains in the region. Raymond III quickly became embroiled in the conflicts of the time.\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Third Crusade alongside figures such as Richard the Lionheart and Philip II of France. His contributions included providing logistics and troops to the crusader forces. Despite the mixed results of the crusade Raymond III managed to secure some territory and influence. His strategic alliances and military actions were crucial in maintaining the independence of Tripoli from Ayyubid expansion.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nOne of the major conflicts during Raymond III’s reign was the struggle against the Ayyubid ruler al-Adil who sought to extend his influence over the Levant. Raymond III along with the rulers of other Crusader States attempted to resist these advances. His military campaigns often focused on defending Tripoli and its surrounding areas from Ayyubid forces. His strategies included fortifying key locations and forming temporary alliances with other Christian factions.\n\nRaymond III was also known for his diplomatic efforts. He maintained relations with various European powers and the Papacy which provided him with some level of political support and resources. His ability to navigate the complex political landscape of the time was vital to the survival of the County of Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nIn 1202 Raymond III participated in the Siege of Sidon which was a joint effort between the Crusader States and the Kingdom of Armenia. The siege aimed to recapture the city from the Ayyubids which had been taken a few years earlier. However Raymond III died during the siege under unclear circumstances. Some accounts suggest he was killed in battle while others propose he may have succumbed to illness. His death marked the end of the direct line of the House of Toulouse in Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy lies in his efforts to preserve the County of Tripoli despite the overwhelming challenges posed by the Ayyubids. He managed to keep the county independent and maintain its influence in the region for a brief period. His strategic military and diplomatic actions provided some stability in an otherwise turbulent era.\n\nFollowing Raymond III’s death the County of Tripoli passed to his sister Alice of Champagne who was the wife of Henry II of Champagne. This transition marked the beginning of a new era for the county under different leadership. The end of the House of Toulouse left a significant impact on the political and military dynamics of the Crusader States.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00034957972820848227}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.023475797846913338}, {"token":